\section{5. Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\subsection{Disadvantages of \(k\)-Nearest Neighbours Regression}
\label{subsec:knn-disadvantages}

Although \(k\)-NN regression is attractive for its simplicity and its non‑parametric, lazy‑learning nature, several practical and theoretical limitations arise from the way the algorithm operates.  In this section we elaborate on the five most important drawbacks that follow directly from the raw observations listed above.

\subsubsection{Bias–Variance Trade‑off Governed by \(k\)}
For a given query point \(\mathbf{x}\) the \(k\)-NN predictor is  
\[
\hat{y}(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}_k(\mathbf{x})} y_i .
\]
The expected squared error can be decomposed as  
\[
\mathbb{E}\!\big[(\hat{y}(\mathbf{x})-f(\mathbf{x}))^{2}\big] 
      = \underbrace{\big(\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}(\mathbf{x})]-f(\mathbf{x})\big)^{2}}_{\text{Bias}^{2}}
        + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}\!\big[(\hat{y}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}(\mathbf{x})])^{2}\big]}_{\text{Variance}}
        + \sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon},
\]
where \(f\) is the true regression function and \(\sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon}\) denotes irreducible noise.  Choosing a **small** \(k\) makes the neighbourhood narrow, so the estimator follows the data closely (low bias) but the average is taken over few noisy observations (high variance).  Conversely, a **large** \(k\) smooths over many points, reducing variance at the expense of higher bias (the prediction may overshoot local structure).  Hence the user must balance these opposing forces, and there is no universal rule for the optimal \(k\).

\subsubsection{Sensitivity to Outliers and Noise}
Because the prediction is a simple arithmetic mean of the raw target values of the nearest neighbours, any anomalous observation that lies among the \(k\) closest points can exert a disproportionate influence.  If an outlier has response \(y_{\text{out}}\) that deviates markedly from the surrounding values, the resulting prediction becomes  
\[
\hat{y}(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{k}\bigl( y_{\text{out}} + \sum_{j\neq\text{out}} y_j \bigr),
\]
so even a single outlier can shift \(\hat{y}(\mathbf{x})\) by \(\frac{1}{k}(y_{\text{out}}-\bar y_{\text{rest}})\).  The effect diminishes only when \(k\) is large, which—as noted above—introduces bias.  Moreover, the distance‑based neighbourhood selection itself is vulnerable: noisy features can alter Euclidean (or other) distances, causing the algorithm to select inappropriate neighbours.

\subsubsection{Computational Burden at Prediction Time}
\(k\)-NN is a **lazy** learner: all training instances \(\{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N}\) are retained, and no model is fitted ahead of time.  To predict at a single query point the algorithm must compute the distance  
\[
d(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}_i)=\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_i\|_2 \qquad i=1,\dots,N,
\]
which entails \(\mathcal{O}(N)\) operations.  For \(Q\) queries the total cost is \(\mathcal{O}(QN)\).  When \(N\) is large (e.g., millions of training records) the naïve approach becomes prohibitive both in time and memory, motivating the use of space‑partitioning structures (KD‑trees, ball‑trees) or approximate‑nearest‑neighbour methods that trade exactness for speed.

\subsubsection{Curse of Dimensionality}
In high‑dimensional spaces the notion of “neighbourhood’’ degrades because distances concentrate.  Let \(\mathbf{x}\) and \(\mathbf{x}'\) be drawn i.i.d. from a distribution with bounded support.  For a Euclidean metric,
\[
\frac{\mathbb{E}[\max_{i} d(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}_i)]}
     {\mathbb{E}[\min_{i} d(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}_i)]}
     \;\xrightarrow[d\to\infty]{}\; 1,
\]
so all points become almost equidistant, and the algorithm loses its ability to discriminate truly “near’’ neighbours from distant ones.  Consequently, the effective neighbourhood expands, inflating bias and deteriorating predictive performance.  Dimensionality reduction or feature selection is often required before applying \(k\)-NN in such settings.

\subsubsection{Impact of Region Imbalance}
Although we focus on regression, the same geometric intuition applies to classification and to the density of training points in different regions of the input space.  If the data are **imbalanced**, i.e., some regions contain far more samples than others, the nearest‑neighbour search will preferentially select points from the dense region.  For a query \(\mathbf{x}\) situated near the boundary between a dense and a sparse region, the majority of its \(k\) neighbours will belong to the dense side, biasing the averaged prediction toward that region’s local mean.  Formally, let \(p_{\text{dense}}\) and \(p_{\text{sparse}}\) denote the local sampling densities and \(\mu_{\text{dense}},\mu_{\text{sparse}}\) the corresponding conditional means.  The expected prediction is approximately
\[
\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}(\mathbf{x})] \approx 
\frac{p_{\text{dense}}\,\mu_{\text{dense}} + p_{\text{sparse}}\,\mu_{\text{sparse}}}
     {p_{\text{dense}} + p_{\text{sparse}}},
\]
so when \(p_{\text{dense}}\) dominates the estimate is pulled toward \(\mu_{\text{dense}}\), irrespective of the true underlying function at \(\mathbf{x}\).

\subsubsection{Summary}
The five limitations above—(i) the bias–variance dependence on \(k\), (ii) vulnerability to outliers and noise, (iii) linear‑in‑\(N\) computational cost at query time, (iv) degradation of distance discrimination in high dimensions, and (v) bias introduced by uneven sampling densities—constitute the primary challenges that practitioners must address when deploying \(k\)-NN regression.  Understanding these drawbacks informs the choice of complementary techniques (e.g., robust distance metrics, dimensionality reduction, adaptive \(k\), or hybrid models) that can mitigate the weaknesses while preserving the algorithm’s intuitive appeal.

