%   7. SUMMARY
%---------------------------%
\section{7. Summary}
\label{sec:summary}

\subsection{Parametric vs. Non‑Parametric Learning}
\label{subsec:parametric-nonparametric-summary}

A learning algorithm can be classified according to how the hypothesis space evolves with the amount of training data.

\subsubsection{Parametric models}
A \emph{parametric} model assumes a fixed‑size set of parameters \(\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}\) that completely determines the predictor 
\[
\hat{y}=f(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta}) .
\]
The number of parameters \(p\) does not depend on the number of training examples \(N\).  Classical examples are linear regression \((p=d+1)\), logistic regression, and neural networks with a prescribed architecture.  
Because the model capacity is limited, parametric learners tend to exhibit a bias that is independent of \(N\) but a variance that typically \emph{decreases} as more data are observed.

\subsubsection{Non‑parametric models}
A \emph{non‑parametric} model does not prescribe a fixed functional form.  Formally,
\[
\hat{y}=g_N(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{D}_N),
\]
where \(\mathcal{D}_N=\{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N}\) is the entire training set and the effective number of degrees of freedom is a function of \(N\).  The $k$‑NN regressor is a prototypical non‑parametric learner.

\subsubsection{Bias–Variance intuition}
\[
\operatorname{E}\big[(\hat{y} - y)^2\big] 
      = \underbrace{(\operatorname{Bias}[\hat{y}])^{2}}_{\text{systematic error}}
        + \underbrace{\operatorname{Var}[\hat{y}]}_{\text{estimation error}}
        + \sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon},
\]
where \(\sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon}\) denotes irreducible noise.  
Parametric models usually have **higher bias** but **lower variance** than non‑parametric models; the opposite holds for non‑parametric learners.  Selecting an appropriate modelling family therefore depends on data size, noise level, interpretability requirements, and computational budget.

\subsection{Key Practical Considerations When Building Machine‑Learning Models}
\label{subsec:practical-considerations}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Feature relevance and scaling}\\
    Non‑parametric distance‑based methods (e.g., \(k\)-NN) are extremely sensitive to irrelevant or differently scaled features.  Typical remedies:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Standardisation (zero mean, unit variance) or min‑max normalisation.
        \item Feature selection or dimensionality reduction (PCA, random projections) to reduce the effective dimensionality and alleviate the curse of dimensionality.
    \end{itemize}
\item \textbf{Choice of distance metric}\\
    Euclidean distance is the default, but alternative metrics (Manhattan, Mahalanobis, mixed‑type) may better reflect domain knowledge, especially when features are correlated or of mixed nature.

\item \textbf{Model capacity vs. computational cost}\\
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \emph{Training time}: Parametric learners usually require optimisation (e.g., gradient descent) whereas non‑parametric learners such as \(k\)-NN have essentially no training cost.
        \item \emph{Inference time}: For \(k\)-NN the naïve prediction cost is \(O(Nd)\) per query (distance to all \(N\) points in \(d\) dimensions).  Indexing structures (KD‑trees, ball‑trees) or approximate methods (LSH) can dramatically reduce this cost in low‑to‑moderate dimensions.
    \end{itemize}
\item \textbf{Robustness to noise}\\
    Averaging over many neighbours reduces variance but also blurs sharp local structure.  Weighting schemes (inverse distance, Gaussian kernels) or robust aggregation (median, trimmed mean) can mitigate the influence of outliers.

\item \textbf{Cross‑validation and hyper‑parameter selection}\\
    The neighbourhood size \(k\) and any distance‑metric parameters must be tuned via a validation protocol (e.g., \(K\)-fold cross‑validation).  Proper data splitting avoids optimistic bias caused by reusing the same points for neighbour search and performance assessment.

\item \textbf{Curse of dimensionality}\\
    In high dimensions distances concentrate, eroding the notion of a “nearest’’ neighbour.  Formally,
    \[
    \frac{d(\mathbf{x},\text{nearest})}{d(\mathbf{x},\text{farthest})}\;\xrightarrow[d\to\infty]{}\;1 .
    \]
    Remedies include dimensionality reduction, metric learning, or hybrid pipelines that first learn a low‑dimensional representation (e.g., via a regularised linear model) and then apply \(k\)-NN in the reduced space.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.5cm}
\subsection{k‑Nearest Neighbour Regression}
\label{subsec:knn-summary}

\subsubsection{Definition}
Given a query point \(\mathbf{x}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\) and a training set \(\mathcal{D}_N=\{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N}\), the \emph{k‑NN regressor} predicts
\[
\boxed{\;
\hat{y}(\mathbf{x}_{q}) = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i\in\mathcal N_{k}(\mathbf{x}_{q})} y_i
\;}
\tag{1}
\]
where \(\mathcal N_{k}(\mathbf{x}_{q})\) denotes the indices of the \(k\) training instances with the smallest distance to \(\mathbf{x}_{q}\).

\subsubsection{Weighted variants}
A common refinement assigns larger weights to nearer neighbours:
\[
\hat{y}(\mathbf{x}_{q}) = \frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i\in\mathcal N_{k}(\mathbf{x}_{q})} w_i\,y_i}
                               {\displaystyle\sum_{i\in\mathcal N_{k}(\mathbf{x}_{q})} w_i},
\qquad 
w_i = K\!\bigl(d(\mathbf{x}_{q},\mathbf{x}_i)\bigr),
\]
where \(K(\cdot)\) is a kernel function (e.g., Gaussian \(K(r)=\exp(-r^{2}/2h^{2})\)).  The bandwidth \(h\) controls the degree of smoothing.

\subsubsection{Algorithmic steps}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Distance computation}: evaluate \(d(\mathbf{x}_{q},\mathbf{x}_i)\) for all \(i\).
\item \textbf{Neighbour selection}: select the \(k\) smallest distances.
\item \textbf{Aggregation}: compute the (weighted) average of the selected targets using (1) or its weighted analogue.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Worked example (univariate)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c}
$x_i$ & $y_i$ \\ \hline
1.0 & 2.0 \\
2.0 & 2.5 \\
3.0 & 3.5 \\
4.0 & 5.0 \\
5.0 & 4.5
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Predict at \(x_q = 3.4\) with \(k=3\).

\begin{enumerate}
\item Distances: \(2.4,\,1.4,\,0.4,\,0.6,\,1.6\).
\item Nearest indices: \(3,4,2\).
\item Unweighted prediction: \(\displaystyle \hat{y}= \frac{3.5+5.0+2.5}{3}=3.67\).
\item With Gaussian weighting (\(h=0.5\)) the weighted prediction is \(\approx 4.03\), illustrating how nearby points receive larger influence.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Questions}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Q\arabic*.}, leftmargin=*, itemsep=12pt]
\item \textbf{Conceptual comparison (easy).}\\
Explain why a parametric model typically exhibits higher \emph{bias} but lower \emph{variance} than a non‑parametric model.  Illustrate your answer with a concrete comparison between linear regression (parametric) and \(k\)-NN regression (non‑parametric), referencing the bias–variance decomposition presented earlier.

\item \textbf{Bias–variance trade‑off for \(k\)-NN (medium).}\\
Consider the univariate true relationship \(y = \sin(\pi x) + \varepsilon\), \(\varepsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,0.1^{2})\), with training points drawn uniformly from \([0,1]\).
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Sketch qualitatively how the expected bias\(^2\) and variance of a \(k\)-NN regressor evolve as \(k\) increases from \(1\) to \(N\).
\item If the primary objective is to obtain \emph{smooth} predictions rather than the smallest pointwise error, which region of \(k\) would you select and why?
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Feature scaling impact (easy).}\\
A 3‑dimensional dataset has feature ranges \([0,1]\), \([0,100]\) and \([0,0.01]\).  
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Show quantitatively why Euclidean distance is dominated by the second feature when no preprocessing is applied.
\item Propose a preprocessing pipeline (including scaling/normalisation steps) that equalises the contribution of each feature to the distance computation.
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Mahalanobis distance for correlated attributes (medium).}\\
Two continuous features \(x_{1},x_{2}\) are highly correlated.  
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Discuss why plain Euclidean distance may be sub‑optimal for \(k\)-NN regression in this situation.
\item Derive the Mahalanobis distance for the two‑dimensional case using the covariance matrix \(\Sigma\) and explain how it compensates for correlation.
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Computational analysis (hard).}\\
Assume a training set of size \(N=10^{5}\) and dimensionality \(d=20\).  
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Estimate the number of elementary arithmetic operations required to answer a single query using the naïve \(k\)-NN algorithm (ignore constant‑factor overhead).
\item If a KD‑tree is employed, give the asymptotic query complexity and discuss for which values of \(d\) the tree actually provides a speed‑up over the naïve approach.
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Cross‑validation design (medium).}\\
Design a \(K\)-fold cross‑validation procedure to tune jointly the neighbourhood size \(k\) and the Gaussian bandwidth \(h\) in a weighted \(k\)-NN regressor.  Specify:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item A sensible grid of candidate \((k,h)\) pairs.
\item How to aggregate the validation errors across folds in order to select the optimal pair \((k^{*},h^{*})\).
\item Practical tricks to keep the computational cost reasonable.
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Curse of dimensionality illustration (hard).}\\
Derive the limit
\[
\frac{d(\mathbf{x},\text{nearest})}{d(\mathbf{x},\text{farthest})}
\;\longrightarrow\;1 \quad\text{as } d\to\infty
\]
for points drawn i.i.d. from a standard \(d\)-dimensional Gaussian distribution.  Explain why this erodes the notion of “nearest neighbour’’ and list at least two remedies mentioned in the theory.

\item \textbf{Weighted \(k\)-NN calculation (easy).}\\
Using the training data from the worked example (Section~\ref{sec:worked-example}), recompute the prediction at \(x_{q}=3.4\) with a Gaussian kernel of bandwidth \(h=0.3\).  
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Show the three weights \(w_i\) for the selected neighbours.
\item Compute the weighted prediction \(\hat y_{\text{w}}(3.4)\) and compare it with the unweighted result \(3.67\).
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Simple \(k\)-NN prediction (easy).}\\
Given the training set \(\{(2,4),\;(5,8),\;(8,12),\;(11,16)\}\), predict the target for a new point \(x=7\) with \(k=3\) using ordinary (unweighted) \(k\)-NN regression.  Show the distance calculations, neighbour selection and the final prediction.

\item \textbf{Model‑selection scenario (medium).}\\
For a medical dataset with \(N=500\) patients and \(d=50\) laboratory measurements (many redundant), discuss whether a parametric model (e.g., regularised linear regression) or a non‑parametric model (e.g., \(k\)-NN) is more appropriate.  Consider bias‑variance trade‑off, interpretability, and computational constraints, and outline a concrete workflow (pre‑processing, model choice, hyper‑parameter tuning).

\item \textbf{Hybrid approaches (hard).}\\
Describe a pipeline that combines parametric and non‑parametric learners (for example, using a parametric model for dimensionality reduction before applying \(k\)-NN).  Discuss the potential advantages (e.g., reduced variance, speed) and possible pitfalls (e.g., error propagation, loss of flexibility).

\item \textbf{Evaluation of neighbourhood size (easy).}\\
A validation experiment yields the following mean‑squared‑error (MSE) values for different \(k\):
\[
k = 1, 5, 10, 20, 50 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{MSE}= 1.20,\;0.85,\;0.70,\;0.68,\;0.72 .
\]
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Analyse the trend in bias and variance that these numbers suggest.
\item Recommend the optimal \(k\) and justify your choice; comment on whether a finer grid around the optimum would be useful.
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Theoretical universality (hard).}\\
Explain why, under standard regularity conditions, the \(k\)-NN regressor with \(k\!\to\!1\) and \(N\!\to\!\infty\) converges to the conditional expectation \(\mathbb{E}[Y\mid\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{x}]\).  Sketch the main steps of the proof (law of large numbers, consistency of the nearest‑neighbour radius, etc.) without entering full technical detail.
\end{enumerate}


